---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import convergenceSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/test/convergence_test.gleam?raw";

const room = excerpt(convergenceSource, "fn room(", "fn channels_of(");
const boardOf = excerpt(convergenceSource, "fn board_of(");
const addRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_adds_keep_both_notes_test(",
);
const voteRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_plus_plus_minus_votes_settle_at_plus_one_test(",
);

const scripted = `// Two adds, then deliver one frame at a time instead of draining.
board_ops.add_note(a.notes, "user-a", "deploys got faster", board.WentWell, 1000, 1)
board_ops.add_note(b.notes, "user-b", "standup stayed short", board.WentWell, 1000, 1)

sluice_js.step(sluice)     // deliver exactly one queued frame
// ... assert the half-delivered board here ...
sluice_js.settle(sluice)   // then drain the rest to quiescence`;

const layout = `retro_tutorial_lustre/
├── gleam.toml                      dependencies and the javascript target
├── package.json                    pnpm build / pnpm serve
├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
├── build.mjs                       esbuild bundle for the browser
├── index.html                      mount point and all of the styling
├── src/
│   ├── doc_schema.gleam            step 02 · title, notes, votes
│   ├── note.gleam                  step 03 · the note record and its codec
│   ├── board.gleam                 step 03 · pure canonical snapshot
│   ├── board_ops.gleam             steps 03 and 04 · the shared writes
│   └── retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam every sheet · connection, update, view
└── test/
    ├── note_codec_test.gleam       round trips, including malformed payloads
    ├── board_test.gleam            ordering, tally joins, unfiled notes
    └── convergence_test.gleam      this sheet · the two races`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="testing"
  description="Step six of the watershed build guide: the sluice is an in-memory sequencer running the production client runtime over an injected transport, so the two races from step one become ordinary gleam test assertions with no server, no browser, and no timing luck."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Close the loop you opened</h2>
    <p>
      Step 01 showed you two races and told you they were already checked in.
      This is where you write them. The board now has everything they need — a
      register OR-map of notes, a tally OR-map of votes, and one module of shared
      writes — so the only missing piece is a way to run two clients without
      running two clients.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>sluice</strong> is that piece: an in-memory sequencer and
      transport that runs the production client runtime, with the same codecs,
      pending queues, resubmit, and reconnect catch-up, and the same sequencing
      module the server uses. It exercises the real client paths. It does not
      claim to reproduce floodgate’s storage, authentication, or network
      boundary — keep a live smoke test for those.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Nothing is delivered until you say so</h2>
    <p>
      That is the whole trick. A client’s submission is processed and sequenced
      synchronously, but the inbound frames it produces sit in a queue.
      <code>settle</code> drains them to quiescence; <code>step</code> delivers
      exactly one. Server order and the order each replica observes it become two
      separate things you control.
    </p>
    <p>
      So a room is: one sluice, two connected documents, a settle to finish both
      handshakes, the channels created on A, and a second settle so B can resolve
      the same handles from the root map.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">test/convergence_test.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={room} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Neither settle is decoration. Drop the second one and B is looking at a
      root map with no channel handles in it yet, so <code>channels_of</code>
      fails on a board that is merely early rather than broken.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Assert on the board, not on the map</h2>
    <p>
      The tests read through <code>board_ops.snapshot</code> — the same function
      the UI renders from. That is deliberate. Asserting on raw OR-map entries
      would pass while the board that people actually look at was sorting its
      columns differently on each replica.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={boardOf} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Which is why <code>board_ops</code> exists as its own module: a test that
      reimplements the write it is testing proves the reimplementation works.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The two races, as tests</h2>
    <p>
      Both notes survive because they were never in the same slot. Both
      assertions matter: <code>board_a |> should.equal(board_b)</code> is
      convergence, and the count and the two lookups are the claim that
      convergence didn’t happen by throwing one note away.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={addRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Then three votes across two clients, settling at <code>+1</code>. Same
      shape, same settle, and no <code>sleep</code> anywhere — this is a
      deterministic assertion, not a race you hope resolves before the timeout.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={voteRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Name the race your story depends on.</strong> A generic
        two-client convergence test can pass while the thing your users care
        about is broken. These two are here because they are the two promises the
        board makes: nothing you type disappears, and nothing you vote is lost.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: script the intermediate state</h3>
    <p>
      When you want to inspect a board mid-flight — one note delivered, the other
      still in the queue — <code>step</code> advances one inbound frame at a
      time. It does not reorder anything already sequenced; submission call order
      decides that.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={scripted} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>More controls.</strong> Both <code>sluice</code> and
        <code>sluice_js</code> provide <code>pause</code> / <code>resume</code>
        {" "}to hold one client’s inbound frames, and <code>advance(ms)</code> to
        move the sequencer’s logical clock for frame timestamps. Presence uses
        the target runtime’s wall clock instead. JavaScript also exposes
        <code>step_info</code> with delivery metadata, which is how the demo on
        step 01 draws its op log.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The finished project</h2>
    <p>
      Six sheets, one source tree. Everything the procedure built, in the order
      it built it:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={layout} lang="text" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Every snippet in this guide that comes from the project is quoted from
      these files at build time, so what you read here and what compiles cannot
      drift. The complete source is in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/tree/main/examples/retro_tutorial_lustre">
        <code>examples/retro_tutorial_lustre</code></a>, and it is meant to be
      copied out and renamed.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code
        code={`cd examples/retro_tutorial_lustre
gleam test`}
        lang="sh"
        theme={watershedCodeTheme}
      />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Where to go when this board gets too small</h2>
    <p>
      The tutorial stops on purpose. It has no sequences, no drag and drop, no
      cross-column moves, no edit or delete, and no vote budgets — every one of
      those is a genuinely harder problem, and stacking them here would have
      buried the two races the board exists to explain.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/examples#retro_board_lustre">advanced retro board</a> is the
      same idea with the hard parts left in: five channels, ordered columns on a
      sequence, cross-channel moves that render honestly rather than pretending
      to be atomic, and edit and delete flows. It is the next thing to read.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beyond it, the <a href="/examples">example gallery</a> maps this loop onto
      thirteen complete browser applications, and the{" "}
      <a href="/structures">field atlas</a> has a page per structure with its
      merge rule and its best-fit uses.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Run <code>gleam test</code> and get <code>8 passed, no failures</code> in
      about a second: two convergence races, the board’s ordering and joins, and
      the note codec — no server, no browser, no timing luck.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
